comments[8]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T17:13:12.017Z","harness:claude-code","Re-verified 2026-07-26 and the gap is unchanged: a grep across every workflow finds exactly two tracker-quality invocations, both of them changelog:pm:check (ci.yml:200 and release.yml:110). No workflow runs pm validate or pm health against this repository's own tracker, so the only data-quality property CI defends is that CHANGELOG.md matches what the generator would emit.\n\nTwo new invariants were established in this repository today and neither has a gate, which sharpens what this item needs to cover:\n\n1. Every terminal item now carries a release identifier derived from the tag containing its close event — 1,782 of 1,795 stamped, the remaining 13 deliberately unstamped because they close after the newest tag. This is what makes a metadata write on a closed item changelog-neutral. Nothing prevents the next release from closing items without a stamp, at which point attribution silently reverts to updated_at for those items and the drag returns one release at a time rather than all at once, which is harder to notice.\n\n2. Every terminal item that has a parent now carries a typed edge to it, and the terminal corpus carries roughly 3,900 evidence-derived relationship edges. Nothing prevents the next closed item from being terminal with zero typed out-edges, so the graph completeness achieved by a bulk pass decays by attrition.\n\nBoth are exactly the shape this item describes — a property that a bulk pass establishes and that only a ratchet keeps. Suggest the gate assert deltas rather than absolutes (no new terminal item without attribution, no new terminal item without a typed edge where a parent exists), so the historical residual lists do not have to be empty for CI to be green.\n"
  "2026-07-26T17:16:40.449Z","harness:claude-code","Correction to the preceding comment, and a sharper statement of the gap. My grep matched the literal string \"pm validate\" and therefore missed the invocations that call the built CLI. CI does run tracker checks, under the static gate in ci.yml:207-216: validate --check-storage-integrity --json, plus history --verify --strict-exit on a single dynamically selected representative item. Those exist for merge safety (pm-iwsj) and the single-stream sampling is the finding pm-pdr8t1 owns.\n\nSo the accurate gap is narrower and worth stating precisely: CI runs the storage-integrity check but runs none of the data-quality checks — no default validate profile, no --check-resolution, no --check-history-drift, and no pm health at all. The live counts those would surface today are validate_resolution_missing_fields:289 and health ok:true with telemetry_queue_pending:5. A ratchet is therefore the right shape rather than a pass/fail gate, exactly as this item already specifies: the 289 is a legacy set owned by pm-wenq and must not have to reach zero for CI to be green.\n\nThe two new invariants noted above still hold and are still ungated — terminal release attribution and terminal typed-edge coverage — and neither is covered by --check-storage-integrity, which validates artifact parseability rather than field completeness.\n"
  "2026-07-29T04:59:49.400Z","harness:claude-code","A live defect escape closes the argument for this item, 2026-07-29. CI runs exactly one tracker check, ci.yml line 206, node dist/cli.js validate --check-storage-integrity --check-history-drift --strict-exit, plus changelog:pm:check at line 187 and again in release.yml line 113. Those are integrity and changelog gates. None of the five data-quality checks - metadata, resolution, lifecycle, files, dependency_references - runs anywhere in CI. What escaped: commit c90adb1ea introduced a scalar parent on seven v1 milestone Epics pointing at pm-j7a, which is correct on its own channel but collides with pre-existing inverted child dependency rows on the same seven pairs. The result was validate_hierarchy_parent_cycle with eight named ids and the sample path pm-2xl to pm-j7a to pm-2xl. That commit passed CI Gates, a further feature commit landed on top of it, the 2026.7.29 release was cut from that tree, and the artifact was published to npm - all with a hierarchy cycle in the tracker and every gate green. It was found only because a human-directed session ran pm validate by hand the next morning. The storage-integrity and history-drift checks cannot catch it by construction: the bytes are well formed and every hash verifies; the defect is semantic. That is the exact gap this item names. Two consequences for the acceptance shape. First, the ratchet must cover the lifecycle and metadata checks specifically, because those are the two that detect contradictions rather than corruption. Second, a warning-count ratchet is the right instrument rather than a pass-fail gate, because the corpus carries known legacy classes that cannot go to zero - today 179 stale linked paths and 291 missing resolution fields, both on terminal holders - and a gate that must be green would simply be disabled. The count of warning CLASSES moved 7 to 4 during today's repair; that number is the ratchet."
  "2026-08-04T14:21:07.639Z","harness:codex","Duplicate check evidence: all-status search found pm-ips23h as the generic measured-population mechanism. This item remains canonical for validate/health warning baselines and hosted/local CI enforcement, so both are implemented through one shared gate."
  "2026-08-04T14:52:20.958Z","harness:codex","Evidence 2026-08-04: implemented a generic shrinking-only tracker measurement gate with exhaustive pm validate warning and pm health check classes, dependency-kind and graph-profile ceilings, terminal-owner retirement, fail-closed update behavior, bounded mutation attribution receipts, and a sandbox negative control. Wired it into quality:static, mandatory CI, and the release gate registry. Verification passed: 432 files / 6836 tests; coverage 100% statements (48644/48644), branches (35239/35239), functions (9955/9955), lines (47149/47149); quality:static; typecheck; docs/links; secret scan; pnpm audit; package artifact gate; 88-command package-first dogfood; packed npx smoke; isolated packed Bun install/init/create/search. The first static run observed a 193ms query import against a 172ms ceiling; unchanged exact rerun and the full suite passed without altering the ceiling, identifying environment variance rather than accepting regression."
  "2026-08-04T15:43:11.134Z","harness:codex","Duplicate-check evidence: all-status searches for clean-clone tracker measurement, merge-driver health, ignored linked paths, PR #894, and DeepScan reuse pm-kpftft for hosted/local ratchet parity; no duplicate clean-clone remediation item exists."
  "2026-08-04T16:11:16.139Z","harness:codex","Review remediation evidence 2026-08-04: hosted CI exposed local clone configuration as hidden state. The gates job now installs the committed merge-driver configuration before pnpm lint and asserts .gitattributes remains unchanged; the later duplicate install was removed. Contract coverage proves ordering and single execution. A fresh clone with the current patch, latest pm-changelog, build, and merge install reports the same 195 missing / 0 orphaned file baseline as the workspace and passes all 19 tracker ratchets. Linked suite passed 98/98; CI-mode quality:static, typecheck, contracts, docs, and audit are green."
  "2026-08-04T16:43:06.164Z","harness:codex","CodeRabbit remediation evidence: the unparsed merge-install bootstrap now uses default human output, and the workflow contract asserts clone preparation precedes both pnpm lint and the explicit tracker-measurement step while occurring exactly once. Focused and full repository gates pass."
